-->DBMS stores data in tables with rows and columns.
-->DBMS is used when structure is fixed and relations are important.
Ex : orders
--> MongoDB is Schema-less (or schema-flexible) Documents in the same collection can have different fields
-->No real joins (only $lookup, which is limited & slower) No foreign key enforcement Relationships must be handled 
manually in application code
// You fetch student
// Then fetch department separately

-->MongoDB stores data in JSON-like documents.

-->MongoDB is used when data is large, schema changes frequently, and the application needs high scalability and performance

-->We chose MongoDB for nested configurations like data inside data. MongoDB allows us to store single document, which avoids 
complex joins and makes read operations faster.
--> Campaigns are primarily read-heavy, retrieved as a whole for execution and analytics, and MongoDB fits that access 
pattern well.
For entities with strict relationships and transactional consistency—like users, billing, and permissions—we still use SQL. 
So the choice was data-driven, not technology-driven.


When to use MongoDB?
------------------------
-->You should use MongoDB when you are building internet and business applications that need to evolve quickly and scale elegantly.
-->MongoDB is popular with developers of all kinds who are building scalable applications using agile methodologies.
MongoDB is a great choice if one needs to:

-->Support a rapid iterative development.
-->Scale to high levels of read and write traffic - MongoDB supports horizontal scaling through Sharding, distributing data across 
several machines, and facilitating high throughput operations with large sets of data.
Scale your data repository to a massive size.
--> Evolve the type of deployment as the business changes.
-->Store, manage and search data with text, geospatial, or time-series dimensions